ing to say is there's light  ahead and we should be optimistic  because of the vaccines as a medium to  long-term way out of this if we look at  israel  we are seeing that once they have gotten  down to vaccinating those in their 20s  and their 30s  they are seeing actually that numbers  are falling and so there is a way to  suppress this virus  and to stop severe disease with our  vaccines however  we are far way off from vaccinating  people in those age groups  hopefully as the health secretary said  by the summer so the question is how do  we ease restrictions in a one-way  process we don't see a third wave over  the next few months while the vaccine's  rolled out  and that requires a strong test trace  isolate system  especially supporting people in  isolating who are testing positive  as well as mass testing to really hit  higher prevalence areas  younger children in scotland and wales  are going to be returning to school  tomorrow return to the classroom  tomorrow it feels like in england the  ambition is to get  all schools back to all pupils on the  8th of march  who do you think has got the right  approach well  i'm obviously biased because i advise  the scottish government on their  education policies and so  i would agree what we've done here which  is tomorrow having the younger kids go  back  and to assess after several weeks what  has been the impact on transmission  what have we seen in schools and we were  learning from denmark who are ahead of  us several weeks and who also have the  new variant  the new variant is creating uncertainty  which means we need to go more  cautiously  so there is a huge risk with bringing  back all kids at the same time and then  having to shut schools again and we're  trying here to move slowly to move  cautiously and  get kids back as many kids as possible  but in a slow  staged phased way and with testing the  other thing i'd mention and it's an easy  win  for i think england is in scotland we  have allowed children  